  ],
  "text": "Two notes sound good together when their frequencies form a simple ratio. That is the whole foundation. It feels like taste. It is arithmetic. Double a frequency and you get the same note, higher. That is an octave. Two to one. Three to two gives you a fifth, which is the next most settled sound in music. The simpler the ratio, the more the waves line up, and the more resolved it sounds. But there is a problem, and every piano in the world is built around it. Stack perfect fifths and you never land back on your starting note. The maths does not close. So we detune everything slightly, by an equal amount, so every key is equally slightly wrong. That is equal temperament, and it is why a piano is deliberately, subtly out of tune everywhere. All of Western music runs on an error we agreed to accept. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
